OpenSSH upgrade integration for the ASUS-managed primary listener

The native ssh.service deliberately remains disabled: ASUS controls its
runtime configuration and startup. Ubuntu's OpenSSH preinst stops this unit
on upgrade, while its postinst only restores enabled units. These dpkg hooks
record an already-active managed listener and restore it after package
configuration and verified executable/version replacement.

The checkpoint in /run/leon-asus-openssh-upgrade-guard is sticky across separate
unpack/configure invocations. No checkpoint is created for an inactive or
unmanaged listener. Restoration requires ASUS sshd_enable=2, inactive primary,
configured openssh-server, safe valid runtime configuration, and active ASUS
control and recovery sockets. It uses one ASUS start_sshd request and verifies
the resulting managed listener. No native unit is enabled; recovery port2222
is not restarted. Hook errors are logged to syslog and return success to dpkg.

Interrupted unpack/configure can be recovered by a later dpkg invocation in
the same boot. Invalid configuration or unavailable dependencies leave a
pending checkpoint. An uncertain start request is recorded as requested and
is never automatically resent. Inspect the checkpoint and systemd journal;
after explicit manual recovery, a later post hook clears an active checkpoint.
Normal reboot discards /run state and retains normal ASUS startup behavior.

ASUS disablement and package removal clear pending state. A concurrent manual
systemctl stop with ASUS still enabled cannot be distinguished from a package
stop after executable replacement; use the ASUS SSH setting for intentional
disablement. These hooks cannot recover after an interrupted transaction until
another applicable dpkg invocation runs. They are not a background watchdog.

The dpkg conffile checks for the executable before invoking it so package
removal without purge cannot leave a broken hook command. There are no package
maintainer scripts, activation hooks, credential changes, or private data.
